WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.295
Lesson 5.3.

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:05.805
Oracles, TWAPs & manipulation.

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:08.366
By the end, you'll know exactly which price

00:00:08.366 --> 00:00:11.277
feed actually secures each of your positions, and

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.140
precisely how each one can fail.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.730
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.067
Push versus pull oracles, the two genuinely different

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:22.025
ways a price actually gets into a contract.

00:00:22.025 --> 00:00:24.836
What a T.W.A.P. actually trades off,

00:00:24.836 --> 00:00:26.960
and exactly why it lags.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.497
The specific ways an oracle can fail:

00:00:29.497 --> 00:00:33.870
stale prices, thin-market manipulation, and misconfiguration.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.535
And a full worked example, using the

00:00:36.535 --> 00:00:40.425
source material's own thirty-minute T.W.A.P. scenario.

00:00:41.725 --> 00:00:43.700
Why contracts need an oracle at all.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.045
Here's the one fact this entire lesson builds from.

00:00:48.045 --> 00:00:51.343
A smart contract genuinely cannot see market prices

00:00:51.343 --> 00:00:53.689
on its own; an oracle is what brings

00:00:53.689 --> 00:00:55.955
that price in from the outside.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:59.085
And critically, a lending market liquidates you based

00:00:59.085 --> 00:01:02.216
specifically on that oracle's own price, not whatever

00:01:02.216 --> 00:01:04.593
number happens to be showing on your own

00:01:04.593 --> 00:01:07.433
screen, or on any exchange you personally happen

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.520
to be watching at that exact moment.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.954
Here's the actual difference between the

00:01:12.954 --> 00:01:15.075
two ways that price gets delivered.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:18.097
A push oracle has a network updating prices on

00:01:18.097 --> 00:01:21.955
its own schedule, or specifically whenever price moves past

00:01:21.955 --> 00:01:25.405
a set deviation threshold, whichever comes first.

00:01:25.405 --> 00:01:29.589
A pull oracle instead fetches, and cryptographically verifies, a

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:33.064
price only at the exact moment a transaction actually

00:01:33.064 --> 00:01:37.055
needs it, rather than continuously updating in the background.

00:01:38.355 --> 00:01:39.445
Quick check.

00:01:39.445 --> 00:01:42.290
Which price actually triggers a DeFi liquidation?

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.179
The answer: the oracle price the protocol

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:51.531
itself uses, never the price you happen

00:01:51.531 --> 00:01:53.530
to be watching on your own screen.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.525
What a TWAP actually trades off.

00:01:57.825 --> 00:02:01.085
Here's exactly what a T.W.A.P., a time-weighted

00:02:01.085 --> 00:02:03.938
average price, is actually doing, and why

00:02:03.938 --> 00:02:06.480
it's deliberately built this way.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.844
Instead of reading one single, instantaneous price,

00:02:09.844 --> 00:02:12.173
it averages price out across a real

00:02:12.173 --> 00:02:14.737
window of time, say thirty minutes.

00:02:14.737 --> 00:02:17.942
That averaging is precisely what makes it genuinely

00:02:17.942 --> 00:02:21.447
harder to manipulate with one quick, isolated trade.

00:02:21.447 --> 00:02:23.944
But that exact same averaging is also

00:02:23.944 --> 00:02:26.112
what makes it lag behind a real,

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:28.018
fast market move, on purpose.

00:02:29.318 --> 00:02:31.882
Worth being precise about why this specific

00:02:31.882 --> 00:02:34.447
trade-off exists at all, rather than simply

00:02:34.447 --> 00:02:37.078
reading the current spot price directly.

00:02:37.078 --> 00:02:39.432
A spot price can be manipulated in

00:02:39.432 --> 00:02:42.054
a single block, by one large, isolated

00:02:42.054 --> 00:02:44.708
trade, especially in a thin market.

00:02:44.708 --> 00:02:49.447
A T.W.A.P. makes that same manipulation require sustaining an

00:02:49.447 --> 00:02:54.186
artificial price across the entire averaging window, which is

00:02:54.186 --> 00:02:58.008
genuinely far more expensive to actually pull off.

00:02:59.308 --> 00:03:00.398
Quick check.

00:03:00.398 --> 00:03:04.033
What's the actual trade-off of using a T.W.A.P.?

00:03:08.453 --> 00:03:11.716
The answer: it's genuinely harder to manipulate

00:03:11.716 --> 00:03:13.823
than a spot price, but it lags

00:03:13.823 --> 00:03:16.678
behind fast, real market moves, by design.

00:03:17.978 --> 00:03:19.793
Three ways an oracle can fail.

00:03:21.093 --> 00:03:22.959
Here's each of these three, and

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:25.474
exactly how each one actually happens.

00:03:25.474 --> 00:03:28.979
Stale prices happen specifically during an outage, when the

00:03:28.979 --> 00:03:32.076
feed simply stops updating, but the last known price

00:03:32.076 --> 00:03:34.654
is still what the contract keeps using.

00:03:34.654 --> 00:03:39.104
Thin-market manipulation happens when an attacker moves a low-liquidity

00:03:39.104 --> 00:03:43.431
asset's price artificially, specifically because it doesn't take much

00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:46.389
capital to move a thin market meaningfully.

00:03:46.389 --> 00:03:49.826
And misconfigured feeds happen when a protocol simply

00:03:49.826 --> 00:03:52.435
points at the wrong oracle, or the wrong

00:03:52.435 --> 00:03:55.299
specific parameters, entirely by human error.

00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:00.759
Worth being precise about why thin-market manipulation specifically

00:04:00.759 --> 00:04:04.424
threatens lenders, not just the manipulator themselves.

00:04:04.424 --> 00:04:07.318
An attacker can artificially push up a thinly

00:04:07.318 --> 00:04:10.464
traded token's price, deposit it as collateral at

00:04:10.464 --> 00:04:13.359
that inflated price, and borrow far more than

00:04:13.359 --> 00:04:16.631
the token is actually, genuinely worth, leaving the

00:04:16.631 --> 00:04:20.154
lending protocol holding collateral worth a fraction of

00:04:20.154 --> 00:04:21.979
the debt it's meant to cover.

00:04:23.279 --> 00:04:26.445
Worth being precise about the actual danger inside a

00:04:26.445 --> 00:04:29.970
stale price specifically, since it's the quietest of these

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.659
three failures, right up until it isn't.

00:04:32.659 --> 00:04:35.754
During an outage, the contract keeps using the last

00:04:35.754 --> 00:04:38.909
price it ever received, treating it as current, with

00:04:38.909 --> 00:04:42.244
no warning built in that anything's actually wrong.

00:04:42.244 --> 00:04:45.683
A real market move happening during exactly that outage

00:04:45.683 --> 00:04:49.860
simply isn't reflected anywhere, until the feed eventually recovers.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.250
Quick check.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:56.055
Why is thin-market collateral specifically dangerous for lenders?

00:05:00.475 --> 00:05:03.031
The answer: its price can be artificially

00:05:03.031 --> 00:05:06.257
manipulated, letting someone borrow far more against

00:05:06.257 --> 00:05:08.630
it than it's actually, genuinely worth.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:10.935
Worked example.

00:05:12.235 --> 00:05:14.398
Here's the scenario, exactly as the

00:05:14.398 --> 00:05:16.380
source material lays it out.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:20.204
Your collateral is priced by a thirty-minute T.W.A.P.

00:05:20.204 --> 00:05:23.391
The market itself drops ten percent, in just

00:05:23.391 --> 00:05:26.240
five minutes, a genuinely fast move.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.771
Your own screen shows that

00:05:27.771 --> 00:05:30.055
full ten percent drop, immediately.

00:05:30.055 --> 00:05:33.290
The oracle, still averaging across its thirty-minute

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:35.975
window, might show something closer to just

00:05:35.975 --> 00:05:38.355
two percent, at that same exact moment.

00:05:39.655 --> 00:05:42.287
Worth being precise about the actual trap

00:05:42.287 --> 00:05:44.730
hiding inside this exact gap, since it

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.035
cuts in both directions at once.

00:05:47.035 --> 00:05:50.580
You might genuinely feel liquidated, watching that ten percent

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.281
drop on your own screen, when the oracle hasn't

00:05:53.281 --> 00:05:55.945
caught up yet, and you technically aren't.

00:05:55.945 --> 00:05:58.469
But you also can't rely on that same

00:05:58.469 --> 00:06:01.879
lag lasting: the T.W.A.P. keeps averaging, and it

00:06:01.879 --> 00:06:04.812
does eventually catch up to the real move,

00:06:04.812 --> 00:06:07.540
whether you've prepared for that or not.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:10.995
Watching the oracle actually catch up.

00:06:12.295 --> 00:06:16.155
Here's what that catch-up actually looks like, plotted across

00:06:16.155 --> 00:06:20.326
the full thirty-minute window, in a simplified, illustrative model

00:06:20.326 --> 00:06:23.427
of how a rolling average genuinely converges.

00:06:23.427 --> 00:06:25.741
Right after the drop, at five minutes,

00:06:25.741 --> 00:06:28.766
the oracle's showing roughly negative two percent,

00:06:28.766 --> 00:06:32.032
matching the source material's own figure closely.

00:06:32.032 --> 00:06:34.677
By fifteen minutes, about negative five.

00:06:34.677 --> 00:06:36.664
And by the full thirty minutes, it's

00:06:36.664 --> 00:06:38.652
essentially caught all the way up to

00:06:38.652 --> 00:06:40.532
the real negative ten percent move.

00:06:41.832 --> 00:06:44.266
Notice that the oracle line never stops

00:06:44.266 --> 00:06:46.578
moving toward the real price; it just

00:06:46.578 --> 00:06:48.642
takes real time to get there.

00:06:48.642 --> 00:06:51.457
That's the entire lag, laid out visually: not

00:06:51.457 --> 00:06:54.027
a permanent gap, and not a loophole, just

00:06:54.027 --> 00:06:56.720
a delay that closes steadily, the whole way

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.107
from the moment the real move happened.

00:07:00.407 --> 00:07:02.639
Here's how to actually find this out for

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:05.172
a protocol you use, rather than assuming.

00:07:05.172 --> 00:07:08.848
Check its documentation specifically for which oracle it uses,

00:07:08.848 --> 00:07:11.482
and whether that oracle is push or pull.

00:07:11.482 --> 00:07:13.876
Find the actual TWAP window length, if

00:07:13.876 --> 00:07:15.957
one is used, in real minutes.

00:07:15.957 --> 00:07:18.125
And check whether that window is published

00:07:18.125 --> 00:07:20.041
clearly, or whether you'd have to dig

00:07:20.041 --> 00:07:22.007
through the contract itself to find it.

00:07:23.307 --> 00:07:25.874
Here's the exact same lag mechanism, but

00:07:25.874 --> 00:07:28.691
working in the opposite direction, against a

00:07:28.691 --> 00:07:31.432
thinly traded collateral token instead.

00:07:31.432 --> 00:07:33.670
An attacker pushes that token's price

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:35.972
up artificially, on a thin market.

00:07:35.972 --> 00:07:38.750
If the protocol's oracle hasn't caught up yet,

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.878
or if its window is short enough to

00:07:40.878 --> 00:07:43.952
reflect that push quickly, the attacker can deposit

00:07:43.952 --> 00:07:46.611
at the inflated price and borrow well beyond

00:07:46.611 --> 00:07:48.562
what the token is actually worth.

00:07:49.862 --> 00:07:52.957
Here's the one sentence this entire worked example

00:07:52.957 --> 00:07:55.748
exists to prove, worth holding onto more than

00:07:55.748 --> 00:07:59.022
the specific ten-percent and two-percent figures.

00:07:59.022 --> 00:08:02.697
Size your own buffers for the oracle's catch-up, never

00:08:02.697 --> 00:08:06.305
for the lag lasting forever, since a T.W.A.P. smooths

00:08:06.305 --> 00:08:10.247
manipulation, it doesn't erase the real move underneath it.

00:08:11.547 --> 00:08:13.007
Here's your checklist.

00:08:13.007 --> 00:08:16.141
Do it for every collateral type you actually use.

00:08:16.141 --> 00:08:18.355
Know the oracle type and its actual

00:08:18.355 --> 00:08:20.570
source, for each one, not just that

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:22.346
an oracle exists at all.

00:08:22.346 --> 00:08:26.606
Avoid, or deliberately cap, thin-market collateral specifically.

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:29.034
And size your own buffers assuming the

00:08:29.034 --> 00:08:32.023
oracle genuinely catches up, never assuming its

00:08:32.023 --> 00:08:34.576
lag will simply protect you indefinitely.

00:08:35.876 --> 00:08:37.121
Let's recap.

00:08:37.121 --> 00:08:39.969
A contract can't see market prices; an oracle

00:08:39.969 --> 00:08:42.631
brings them in, and it's the oracle price,

00:08:42.631 --> 00:08:46.151
not your screen, that actually triggers liquidation.

00:08:46.151 --> 00:08:49.274
Push oracles update on a schedule or threshold;

00:08:49.274 --> 00:08:52.176
pull oracles fetch and verify on demand.

00:08:52.176 --> 00:08:54.898
A T.W.A.P. is harder to manipulate,

00:08:54.898 --> 00:08:57.617
but lags fast moves, on purpose.

00:08:57.617 --> 00:09:03.177
Oracles fail through stale prices, thin-market manipulation, and misconfiguration.

00:09:03.177 --> 00:09:05.495
And the real rule: size your buffers for

00:09:05.495 --> 00:09:07.757
the catch-up, never for the lag lasting.

00:09:09.057 --> 00:09:12.260
Worth connecting this back to the dependency map from

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.467
this module's own Mastery Starter, one more time.

00:09:15.467 --> 00:09:19.133
The oracle pricing your collateral is another specific link

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:21.883
in that same chain, and now you know exactly

00:09:21.883 --> 00:09:24.266
what to check on it: push or pull, its

00:09:24.266 --> 00:09:26.710
TWAP window if it has one, and how thin

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:29.888
the market behind whatever it's pricing actually is.

00:09:31.188 --> 00:09:34.122
Know each collateral's oracle type and source,

00:09:34.122 --> 00:09:37.055
cap thin-market exposure, and size buffers for

00:09:37.055 --> 00:09:38.978
the catch-up, not the lag.

00:09:38.978 --> 00:09:41.262
Next up, Lesson 5.4: smart

00:09:41.262 --> 00:09:44.477
contracts, state, proxies, admin keys.
